        "content": "<p>tl;dr: I've just realised that I have a fundamental lack of understanding of the typeclass inference algorithm; what transparency level is it running at when it is checking equality of terms which are not themselves instances?</p>\n<hr>\n<p>I have a basic question about transparency levels; I'm trying to understand a trace. I have the following options set:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">profiler</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">useHeartbeats</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">profiler</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Meta</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">synthInstance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and I'm trying to debug a typeclass synthesis failure.</p>\n<p>The failure starts here:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Meta</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">synthInstance</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">38424892.000000</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">❌️</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HeightOneSpectrum</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">              </span><span class=\"n\">SetLike</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Submodule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">↥</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">adicCompletionIntegers</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Extension</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">adicCompletion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">                </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Extension</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">adicCompletion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▶</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and the answer is supposed to be <code>foo</code>, which I have explicitly put into the typeclass system. The attempt to apply it is here</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">616922.000000</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">❌️</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HeightOneSpectrum</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">                </span><span class=\"n\">SetLike</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Submodule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">↥</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">adicCompletionIntegers</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Extension</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">adicCompletion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">150</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▶</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and I would like to understand why this is failing. After lots of unfolding I run into goals like this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">5785.000000</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">❌️</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">✝¹</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=?=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">algebraMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">↥</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">adicCompletionIntegers</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"bp\">✝</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">adicCompletion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"bp\">✝</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toMonoidWithZeroHom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">✝¹</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">▶</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and now the trace is just labelled by <code>[]</code> so I'm a bit unclear about which part of the system is even asking this question. I believe I can reproduce that equality in a standalone file and observe that <code>rfl</code> works, but <code>with_reducible_and_instances rfl</code> fails.</p>\n<p>My question is whether the failure of <code>with_reducible_and_instances rfl</code> explains why the typeclass search fails. Hopefully this has a simple answer (or hopefully it's not hard for me to give the extra details needed to give this a simple answer). The reason I am not confident that the answer is simply \"we're doing typeclass inference so yes equality must happen at instance transparency\" is that the actual equalities here are just of terms of some ring, they are nothing to do with instances themselves.</p>",
        "id": 598327836,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1779973945
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Would it be possible to share a mathlib or FLT branch where one can look at the trace? I should also note that the exact answer depends heavily on which exact version of Lean you are on since the defeq changes affect the answer quite a bit.</p>",
        "id": 598328819,
        "sender_full_name": "Julia Markus Himmel",
        "timestamp": 1779974195
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm always using \"the version of Lean which mathlib is using yesterday or today\" thanks to hopscotch, and the problem is that the trace is gigantic (I don't want to put you through it; I am happy to learn how to do thing myself though). It's <code>v4.30.0-rc2</code> or <code>v4.30.0</code>, are these the same?</p>",
        "id": 598331689,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1779974838
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The short anwer is that yes, it is the right mental model that type class inference should only find solutions which fully typecheck at <code>instances</code> transparency. In Lean 4.30, there are still various loopholes which make this not literally true, but I wouldn't rely on them staying open. In dependently typed situations like yours, this will likely mean that mathlib will mark lots of declarations as <code>@[instance_reducible]</code> to ensure that typeclass resolution can unfold them. But like I said, this is a rapidly evolving situation, the <code>@[instance_reducible]</code> attribute isn't even in Lean yet (it's in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/13637\">lean4#13637</a>)</p>",
        "id": 598334400,
        "sender_full_name": "Julia Markus Himmel",
        "timestamp": 1779975447
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks. I realised that probably a perfectly reasonable minimised version of the question is this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i_f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i_g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The question is: if typeclass inference ever finds itself asking <code>i_f =?= i_g</code> (an equality of two instances) then my experience is that it might well end up asking <code>f x✝ y✝ =?= g x✝ y✝</code> (an equality of two non-instances). My question is simply: if I want <code>i_f =?= i_g</code> to be solved by typeclass inference, should I make sure that <code>f x y = g x y</code> is proved by <code>with_reducible_and_instances rfl</code> even though <code>f x y</code> isn't an instance? And I think that you're saying that the answer is \"if you want to future-proof your code then yes\".</p>",
        "id": 598337184,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1779976075
    }
